      "message": "We can make the code easier to read and maintain by renaming some variables. Gemini feedback:\n\n----\n\n### 1. `updater.cc` - `DownloadPage`\n\n**The Issue: Consecutive Numbering (`res`, `res2`)**\nWhen you see a number appended to a variable name, it usually means the variable lacks semantic meaning. In this function, the code executes a two-step hardware sequence (write to SRAM, then commit to flash), but the variables obscure that intent.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\nconst auto res \u003d SendBulkDataOut(/* ... WriteBin ... */);\n// ...\nconst auto res2 \u003d SendBulkDataOut(/* ... CodeBin ... */);\nif (!res2) FT_LOGE(\"Code bin error\");\nreturn res2;\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Name them based on the distinct operations they represent.\n\n```cpp\nconst auto sram_write_res \u003d SendBulkDataOut(/* ... WriteBin ... */);\n// ...\nconst auto flash_commit_res \u003d SendBulkDataOut(/* ... CodeBin ... */);\nif (!flash_commit_res) FT_LOGE(\"Code bin error\");\nreturn flash_commit_res;\n\n```\n\n### 2. `updater.cc` - `DownloadBin`\n\n**The Issue: Domain Terminology Mismatch (`frame`)**\nThroughout the firmware update logic, the codebase correctly uses the hardware term \"Page\" (e.g., `ErasePage`, `DownloadPage`, `kFocalConfigSize` representing a page size). However, inside the loop tracking the download progress, the variables suddenly switch to using the word \"frame\".\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\n[[maybe_unused]] size_t frame \u003d 0;\n[[maybe_unused]] size_t total_frame \u003d DivRoundUp(file_len, kFocalConfigSize);\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Keep the domain language consistent.\n\n```cpp\n[[maybe_unused]] size_t current_page \u003d 0;\n[[maybe_unused]] size_t total_pages \u003d DivRoundUp(file_len, kFocalConfigSize);\n\n```\n\n### 3. `updater.cc` - `UpdateFw`\n\n**The Issue: Misleading Semantics (`target_addr`)**\nThe variable `target_addr` is initialized directly from `hw_map.config_address`. In embedded systems, \"target address\" usually implies the destination of the firmware executable payload (the run address). By calling the configuration block address `target_addr`, it creates cognitive dissonance for the reader, especially when passed into `ResetMcu(device, target_addr)`.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\nconst uint32_t target_addr \u003d hw_map.config_address;\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Use the exact name of the data it holds to prevent ambiguity.\n\n```cpp\nconst uint32_t config_addr \u003d hw_map.config_address;\n\n```\n\n### 4. `libusb_transport.cc` - Multiple Functions\n\n**The Issue: Overly Terse C-style Variables (`r`, `actual`, `r_open`)**\nBecause this code was ported from a C codebase (Zephyr), it retained some legacy C naming habits. `r` is frequently used for return codes, and `actual` is used for pointer-out parameters. Google C++ Style strongly discourages abbreviations and single-letter variables outside of tight loops.\n\n**Current (in `Open`):**\n\n```cpp\nconst int r_open \u003d libusb_open(device_.get(), \u0026raw_handle);\n// ...\nconst int r_auto \u003d libusb_set_auto_detach_kernel_driver(handle_.get(), 1);\n\n```\n\n**Current (in `Send` / `Recv`):**\n\n```cpp\nint actual \u003d 0;\nconst int r \u003d libusb_bulk_transfer(..., \u0026actual, ...);\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Use descriptive names that clarify what the integer represents.\n\n```cpp\n// In Open:\nconst int open_status \u003d libusb_open(device_.get(), \u0026raw_handle);\nconst int auto_detach_status \u003d libusb_set_auto_detach_kernel_driver(...);\n\n// In Send/Recv:\nint transferred_bytes \u003d 0;\nconst int transfer_status \u003d libusb_bulk_transfer(..., \u0026transferred_bytes, ...);\n\n```\n\n### 5. `ft_util.h` - `DivRoundUp`\n\n**The Issue: Single-Letter Math Variables (`n`, `d`)**\nWhile `n` and `d` are standard mathematical shorthand for numerator and denominator, expanding them makes the template much friendlier to read, especially in a header file that acts as a utility library.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\ntemplate \u003ctypename T\u003e\nconstexpr T DivRoundUp(T n, T d) { ... }\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:**\n\n```cpp\ntemplate \u003ctypename T\u003e\nconstexpr T DivRoundUp(T numerator, T denominator) { ... }\n\n```",
