      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n----\n\n## The Danger of \"Short Reads\"\n\nIn the original patch, `ReceiveBulkDataIn` returned `std::expected\u003cvoid, Error\u003e`. If you requested 64 bytes but the USB layer only returned 10 bytes (a \"short read\"), the function would log a warning, validate the Command Status Wrapper (CSW), and return `Success` (because it didn\u0027t encounter a hard hardware error).\n\n**The problem:** The caller was completely blind to the fact that only 10 bytes were valid.\n\n* In `VerifyData`, the code would happily compute a SHA256 hash across a buffer containing 10 valid bytes and 22 bytes of uninitialized memory.\n* In `GetBootVersion`, it would search for a null terminator in a buffer where the actual string might have been cut off, leading to out-of-bounds reads or garbage text.\n\nBy changing the return type to `std::expected\u003csize_t, Error\u003e`, we force the caller to acknowledge exactly how many bytes are safe to read, strictly enforcing memory safety.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Core API Changes\n\n### `ft_scsi.h`\n\nUpdate the function signature to return `size_t`.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003csize_t, Error\u003e ReceiveBulkDataIn(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, const CmdFormat\u0026 scsi_cmd,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data_buffer, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout_ms);\n\n```\n\n### `ft_scsi.cc`\n\nCapture the exact number of bytes received from the transport layer and return it, completely dropping the deceptive log warning.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003csize_t, Error\u003e ReceiveBulkDataIn(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, const CmdFormat\u0026 scsi_cmd,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data_buffer, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout_ms) {\n  const auto cbw_res \u003d SendCbw(device, scsi_cmd, data_buffer.size(),\n                               TransferDirection::kIn, timeout_ms);\n  if (!cbw_res) return std::unexpected(cbw_res.error());\n\n  size_t bytes_read \u003d 0;\n  if (!data_buffer.empty()) {\n    const auto recv_res \u003d device.Recv(data_buffer, timeout_ms);\n    if (!recv_res) {\n      return std::unexpected(recv_res.error());\n    }\n    bytes_read \u003d *recv_res;\n  }\n\n  const auto csw_res \u003d ReceiveAndValidateCsw(device, *cbw_res, timeout_ms);\n  if (!csw_res) return std::unexpected(csw_res.error());\n\n  return bytes_read;\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Updating the Callers (`updater.cc`)\n\nEvery caller must now explicitly handle the `size_t` value.\n\n### `VerifyData`\n\nWe must guarantee we received exactly enough bytes to form a complete hash.\n\n```cpp\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH\u003e device_hash{};\n\n  const auto io_res \u003d ReceiveBulkDataIn(\n      device,\n      CmdFormat{\n          .opcode \u003d Opcode::kAction,\n          .address \u003d ToLittleEndian(target_addr),\n          .length \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(firmware_data.size())),\n          .subcmd \u003d Subcmd::kVerify,\n          .flags \u003d hash_mode,\n      },\n      device_hash, kVerifyHashTimeout);\n\n  if (!io_res) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Verification command failed over USB.\");\n    return std::unexpected(io_res.error());\n  }\n\n  // NEW: Strict boundary enforcement\n  if (*io_res !\u003d SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Short read during verification. Expected {}, got {}\", \n            SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH, *io_res);\n    return std::unexpected(Error::kHardwareFailure);\n  }\n\n```\n\n### `GetBootVersion`\n\nWe use the returned size to dynamically shrink the memory view before performing the string search.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, Error\u003e GetBootVersion(const UsbDevice\u0026 device,\n                                          DeviceMode current_mode) {\n  const size_t expected_size \u003d (current_mode \u003d\u003d DeviceMode::kBootloader)\n                                   ? kBootVersionMax\n                                   : kRomVersionMax;\n\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, std::max(kBootVersionMax, kRomVersionMax)\u003e data{};\n  auto buffer \u003d std::span(data).first(expected_size);\n\n  const auto res \u003d ReceiveBulkDataIn(\n      device, \n      CmdFormat{.opcode \u003d Opcode::kReadInfo, .subcmd \u003d Subcmd::kReadData}, \n      buffer, kGenericInfoTimeout);\n      \n  if (!res) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Failed to read version info\");\n    return std::unexpected(res.error());\n  }\n\n  // NEW: Shrink the readable span strictly to the valid bytes received\n  auto valid_data \u003d buffer.first(*res);\n  \n  // Safely construct the string\n  const std::string version(valid_data.begin(), \n                            std::ranges::find(valid_data, \u0027\\0\u0027));\n                            \n  FT_LOGI(\"Boot version: {}\", version);\n  return {};\n}\n\n```\n\n### `GetFlashProtection`\n\nEnsure we received at least one byte before evaluating `data[0]`.\n\n```cpp\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, kFlashWpBufferSize\u003e data{};\n\n  const auto res \u003d ReceiveBulkDataIn(\n      device,\n      CmdFormat{.opcode \u003d Opcode::kAction, .subcmd \u003d Subcmd::kFlashWpGet},\n      data, kGenericInfoTimeout);\n      \n  if (!res) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Failed to get flash protect status\");\n    return std::unexpected(res.error());\n  }\n\n  // NEW: Ensure we aren\u0027t reading uninitialized memory\n  if (*res \u003c 1) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Received 0 bytes for flash protect status\");\n    return std::unexpected(Error::kHardwareFailure);\n  }\n\n  const auto wp_area \u003d static_cast\u003cFlashWpArea\u003e(data[0]);\n  FT_LOGI(\"Flash write-protect area \u003d {}\", ToString(wp_area));\n  return wp_area;\n\n```\n\n### `SetFlashProtection`\n\nNo logic changes are needed here. The MCU\u0027s response payload is ignored entirely by this function, so we only care if the transport layer reported success (`!res`).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Updating the Tests (`ft_scsi_test.cc`)\n\nBecause `ReceiveBulkDataIn` now returns a `size_t` payload wrapped in a `std::expected`, the original boolean `EXPECT_TRUE(result.has_value())` tests are insufficient and fail to assert the actual return value.\n\nAny test asserting success on `ReceiveBulkDataIn` needs to be updated. For example:\n\n**Before:**\n\n```cpp\n  auto result \u003d\n      ReceiveBulkDataIn(*device_, scsi_cmd, out_buffer, kScsiCmdTimeOut);\n  EXPECT_TRUE(result.has_value());\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```cpp\n  auto result \u003d\n      ReceiveBulkDataIn(*device_, scsi_cmd, out_buffer, kScsiCmdTimeOut);\n  ASSERT_TRUE(result.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(*result, kUsbBulkMaxPacketSize); \n\n```\n\nYou will need to apply this `ASSERT_TRUE` / `EXPECT_EQ(*result, ...)` pattern to:\n\n* `ReceiveBulkDataIn_Success`\n* `ReceiveBulkDataIn_EmptyBuffer` (expecting `0`)\n* And anywhere else the test relies on the return value of that specific SCSI call.",
